[00:36.32]Conversation One
[00:38.36]M: Tell me, Mrs. Clark, how did you come to be a bearded lady£¿ 
[00:43.11]W: Well, it all began when I started growing a beard, just after my fourth birthday, I believe. 
[00:49.77]M: Really£¿Didn't you see a doctor£¿ 
[00:52.47]W: Oh, yes, my parents took me to dozens of specialists. 
[00:56.85]They just told me to shave it. 
[00:59.08]Well, I was too young to be allowed to use a razor, and electric razors weren't even thought of in those days, so my dad used to shave me once a week before going to church on Sundays. 
[01:11.98]M: And when did you stop shaving£¿
[01:14.37]W: Oh, that would have been when I was around 15. 
[01:18.03]You see it was growing at an enormous rate¡ªsomething like five inches a day. 
[01:23.91]I mean a razor or scissors were no use. 
[01:27.11]It was taking so much time trying to keep it down and I was just wasting my time fighting a losing battle. 
[01:34.76]So I thought...I just let it grow...and that's when I came to work in the circus. 
[01:40.46]I was spotted by a talent scout.
[01:42.75]M: What about the circus£¿ 
[01:44.93]How did you find it at first, being stared at all day£¿
[01:48.83]W: Well, I must admit it was a bit uncomfortable at first¡ªpeople looking at you in surprise with their mouths open. 
[01:56.39]I used to feel like saying ¡°It's all right, dear. It's not that unusual. You know...It's only a bit of extra hair. It's not another head or something.¡± 
[02:07.72]I get used to the pointing and laughing in the end. 
[02:10.84]Even the jokes don't upset me now. 
[02:13.54]But, it's a bit boring in fact, after thirty years, just sitting here all day being stared at. 
[02:20.24]M: OK. Thank you, Mrs. Clark, for sharing your experience with us. 
[02:25.08]I have to say that being yourself is a very admirable act.
[02:29.74]W: You are welcome.
