[17:53.77]Recording Two
[17:55.56]A new study predicts that warmer waters in the Atlantic Ocean will continue to increase the number of major hurricanes. 
[18:04.27]Researchers say each hurricane season is likely to produce five to eight major hurricanes by 2100.
[18:12.54]In 2017, six major hurricanes formed in the Atlantic. 
[18:18.15]Three of them¡ªHarvey, Irma and Maria¡ªmade landfall. 
[18:23.79]The storms hit parts of the United States and Caribbean, causing loss of life and an estimated $265 billion in damages.
[18:34.83]The new study used a computer modeling system to simulate different climate conditions.
[18:42.43]Researchers say they were able to correctly predict the active hurricane season in June 2017.
[18:49.76]Additional experiments showed that the main driver of the 2017 hurricane activity was a much warmer Atlantic Ocean. 
[18:58.47]Hiroyuki Murakami was a lead researcher on the study. 
[19:03.14]He is also a climate scientist and hurricane expert. 
[19:07.05]He told the Associated Press his team found that a combination of natural conditions and man-made climate change are making Atlantic Ocean waters warmer. 
[19:18.00]Man-made causes included the burning of coal, oil and gas. 
[19:22.84]Warm water is important because it acts as a fuel for hurricanes. 
[19:27.67]Water has to be at least 26 ¡æ for a storm to form. 
[19:33.14]The warmer the water, the more a hurricane can resist forces that would cause it to weaken.
[19:39.65]The Atlantic is predicted to warm faster than the rest of the world's oceans. 
[19:44.61]This is why the study estimates that the number of major storms will probably increase by two or more on average. 
[19:52.84]And warm water is especially affecting one important area, Murakami said. 
[19:58.83]This box-shaped area includes a large territory south of Florida and north of South America, extending all the way east to Africa. 
[20:09.02]Some of the strongest Atlantic hurricanes form off the coast of West Africa, before heading west toward the Caribbean and the US East Coast. 
[20:19.67]Ocean water in this box-shaped area averaged 0.4 ¡æ warmer than normal throughout the 2017 season. 
[20:29.79]Murakami said the temperature was very unusual for a six-month time period.
[20:35.23]Some outside experts had issues with parts of Murakami's study. 
[20:40.54]Brian McNoldy is a hurricane researcher at the University of Miami. 
[20:45.64]He told the Associated Press it makes sense that unusually warm water caused the extra hurricane activity in 2017. 
[20:55.03]But he was not willing to completely blame climate change. 
[20:58.77]Kevin Trenberth is with the National Center for Atmospheric Research. 
[21:03.58]He took issue with the fact that the study did not include research on large increases in ocean heat in deeper ocean areas. 
[21:11.91]Trenberth said this can also be caused by climate change.
